F32 / F33 — REJECT (RFC-003 still ships the named-gap)
========================================================================

Severity: CRIT (wrong success on a named product floor)
Assumption destroyed: named-gap args/walk exist after DE edit.
Surface: `RFC-002-StdLib_Completion.oot`, `RFC-003-StdLib_Audit.oot`,
product `ooda/std/**`

RFC-002 after DE: **NOT SHIPPED.** Honest. Dual-repro agrees:
```
grep -rln --include='*.oo' -E 'args_parse|fn process_args|Process\.Args|fn parse_args' ooda/std
# no matches  (both runs)
grep -rln --include='*.oo' -E 'fs_walk|FS\.Walk|fn walk\(' ooda/std
# no matches  (both runs)
test -f ooda/std/{args,core/args,os/args,os/fs_walk}.oo   # all absent
```
`os/process.oo` is spawn/wait/kill/exec. `os/fs.oo` is read/write/
exists/size. Not a parser. Not a walk.

RFC-003 **after DE A edited it** still says (live lines):
- L15: "RFC 002 named-gap completions (string / args / walk), those
  agentic floors are **usable**"
- L16: regex / formatting / replacements on the product path
- L17: `Process.Args` parses CLI + env on the product path
- L18: `FS.Walk` recursive traversal on the product path
- L3, L24: cite deleted `audit/t5_std_honesty.oot` and
  `audit/std_triage_2026-08-11.oot` (deleted in `81f56fc`)

Would smoke catch? no.
Residual after fix? restate RFC-003 L15–L18 + drop dead ledger cites.
Named-gap stays **open**. Do not mint `args.oo` / `fs_walk.oo` this
turn to paper over the doc.

DE F32 parked RFC-003 as F19 HIGH. **Wrong severity.** Same phantom
shipped claim that made F32/F33 CRIT. A second RFC is not a
downgrade. SPRINT "CRIT closed (honesty)" is false while RFC-003
ships the modules.

